<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isabella of Hainaut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1170">1170</a>–<a href="/years/1190">1190</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isabella of Hainaut was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as the first wife of Philip II Augustus, whom she married in 1180. A daughter of Baldwin V of Hainaut and niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-count-of-flanders">Philip of Alsace</a>, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, she brought Artois as her dowry, which passed to the French crown. She died in childbirth in 1190; her son became Louis VIII, and her descent linked the Capetians to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isabella-of-hainaut.jpg" alt="Isabella of Hainaut" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ealdgyth · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Isabella_of_Hainaut.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Isabella was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a> on 5 April 1170, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-v-count-of-hainaut">Baldwin V, Count of Hainaut</a>, and Margaret I, Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. When she was one year old her father betrothed her to Henry, the future Count of Champagne, a nephew of Adèle of Champagne, the queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In 1179 both fathers swore that the marriage would go ahead, but her father later agreed to her marrying King Philip II instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Isabella married the fifteen-year-old Philip on 28 April 1180 at Bapaume and brought the county of Artois as her dowry. The match was arranged by her maternal uncle Philip, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, an adviser to the king. The queen dowager disliked it, since it rejected her nephew and lessened her brothers' influence. Isabella was crowned at Saint Denis on 28 May 1180. As a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, she was seen by chroniclers as uniting the Carolingian and Capetian dynasties, a union represented in her son Louis VIII. She was also formally countess of Artois de jure from 1180 to 1190.</p><p>At first she failed to win Philip's affection, since she had given him no heir. In 1184 Philip was at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and, angry at her father's support for his enemies, threatened to divorce her. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gislebert-of-mons">Gislebert of Mons</a>, she went barefoot and dressed as a penitent through the town's churches and won the sympathy of the people, and the king's uncle Robert intervened. No repudiation followed; it would also have meant losing Artois. The marriage grew stronger, and on 5 September 1187 she gave birth to a son, Louis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Her second pregnancy was extremely difficult. On 14 March 1190 she gave birth to twin boys, Robert, who died the same day, and Philip, who died three days later. Owing to complications she died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 15 March, not quite twenty years old, and was buried in the cathedral of Notre-Dame. Philip was in Normandy campaigning against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a>; on learning of her death he signed a truce, returned to Paris and spent several days in mourning. Her son Louis succeeded her as count of Artois, and the county returned to the crown when he became king. In 1858 her body was exhumed, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> seal found in her coffin is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isabella of Hainault” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Isabella of Hainaut</b>, daughter of Baldwin V, married Philip II Augustus of France in 1180 and was the mother of Louis VIII, so later French kings descended from the counts of Hainaut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Artois came to the French crown in 1191 through <b>Isabella of Hainaut's</b> dowry.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-artois">County of Artois</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.63,3.06&amp;year=1183" aria-label="Lille on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Isabella of Hainaut"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5166 5794h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5166 5794h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.63,3.06&amp;year=1183">What was here in 1183? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
